course: dictionary
lesson: 504
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word ass can mean a donkey, an animal with long ears. It can also mean a silly or foolish person. «You silly ass, Mole!» Here the word silly is the clue, so ass means a foolish person, not an animal.
explain: Look at the words around the word to decide. Is there an animal in the story, or is someone being told off? In the Bible, «Jesus rides into Jerusalem on an ass» is about a real donkey, because the story is about a journey on an animal. In fables, the ass is often foolish, which links the donkey to the silly meaning.
explain: Between friends, calling someone an ass is a mild telling-off, not a cruel insult. Toad's friends say «Don't be an ass!» to show they are cross with him but still his friends. To check your meaning, swap in another word, such as donkey or fool, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: An ass is a donkey, an animal like a small horse with long ears.
fact: Calling someone an ass means they are being stupid or silly.
fact: «Conceited» means too proud of yourself.
fact: «Heedless» means careless, not paying attention to what might go wrong.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning, so always check the context to find the right one.
exercise: In the Bible, Jesus rides into Jerusalem on an ass. What is an ass in this sentence? | A kind of boat | A silly person | *A donkey | A king's crown
exercise: «You silly ass, Mole!» What does ass mean here? | A large field of grass | *A stupid or silly person | A small cart pulled by a horse | A donkey with long ears
exercise: «What a conceited and heedless ass!» Find and copy one word that means careless. | *heedless | and | conceited | What
test: Hannah reads, «The ass plodded slowly up the hill with a sack on its back.» What is the ass? | Someone who is foolish | The hill itself | A horse-drawn carriage | *A donkey
test: Ben says, «Don't be an ass!» is a telling-off. What does it really mean? | Sit down and rest | Please keep your voice down | *Stop being silly or foolish | Go and find a donkey
exam: In «Yes, I've been a conceited old ass... but now I'm going to be a good Toad», what does ass mean? | A long-eared horse pulling a cart | *A foolish person who has admitted his mistakes | A grey donkey eating grass in a field | A list of animals on a farm
